diagnosis_text:
A thorough investigation of the alleged "divorce" between the 20.x (OSPF) side and the 120.x (EIGRP) side found no fault. The redistribution boundary described by the user as the "translator box" is router r11, which runs dual routing protocols: OSPF (area 3, connecting to r1 on the 111.x segment) and EIGRP AS 100 (connecting to r12 on the 112.x segment and r13 on the 103.x segment). R11 is correctly configured with `redistribute eigrp` under its OSPF process, injecting EIGRP-learned prefixes — including 120.0.0.0/8 — into the OSPF domain as external Type-2 LSAs.

Every control-plane indicator is healthy: r11's OSPF neighbor with r1 is Full (112.1.1.1 / 111.1.1.1); both EIGRP adjacencies with r12 (112.1.1.2) and r13 (103.1.1.2) are in Full state; r11 carries 11 OSPF external LSAs and is recognized as an ASBR; r1 has a valid OSPF external route to 120.0.0.0/8 pointing to r11; r11 has a valid EIGRP successor to 120.0.0.0/8 pointing to r12; and r12 uses a default route via r11 to reach all OSPF-domain destinations. FRR logs on r11 show a clean initialization with no errors.

Data-plane confirmation: a live ping from pc0 (20.1.1.10) to pc12 (120.1.1.10) achieved 0% packet loss, and the reverse ping from pc12 to pc0 was equally clean.

Root cause: There is no fault. The redistribution boundary (r11) is functioning correctly and the two address families are communicating normally. The ticket appears to be based on an incorrect assumption — the network was never broken.

Corrective fix: No configuration change is needed. The operator should verify that their monitoring or test tool was functioning correctly before the ticket was raised.
=== END ===